A set can tell us when a number appears for the second time. While visiting the array, store unseen values and save the first duplicate. Then scan the expected range and find the value that was never added.

This uses extra memory, but the two checks are easy to follow. The input array stays unchanged.

```java
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

class Solution {
    public int[] findMissingRepeatingNumbers(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        Set<Integer> seen = new HashSet<>();
        int repeating = -1;

        for (int value : nums) {
            if (!seen.add(value)) {
                repeating = value;
            }
        }

        int missing = -1;
        for (int value = 1; value <= n; value++) {
            if (!seen.contains(value)) {
                missing = value;
                break;
            }
        }

        return new int[] {repeating, missing};
    }
}
```

An important edge case is when the missing value is `n`. The final loop still checks that value because it goes through the full range, not just the values before the duplicate.

Time complexity: `O(n)` on average.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the set.

A frequency array fits nicely because every value is between `1` and `n`. Count each value while scanning `nums`, then check the counts from `1` through `n`. A count of `2` gives the repeated number, and a count of `0` gives the missing number.

Dry run for `nums = [1, 2, 2, 4]`: the counts are `1, 2, 1, 0`, so the result is `[2, 3]`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> findMissingRepeatingNumbers(vector<int> nums) {
        int n = static_cast<int>(nums.size());
        vector<int> frequency(n + 1, 0);

        for (int value : nums) {
            frequency[value]++;
        }

        int repeating = -1;
        int missing = -1;

        for (int value = 1; value <= n; value++) {
            if (frequency[value] == 2) {
                repeating = value;
            } else if (frequency[value] == 0) {
                missing = value;
            }
        }

        return {repeating, missing};
    }
};
```

For the smallest input, `nums = [1]`, the only possible answer is `[1, 2]` only if the range were larger, but here the range is `1..n`, so the valid one-element case is `[1]` and has no missing value. Under the problem's stated condition of one repeated and one missing value, `n` is at least `2`.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the frequency array.

Every number points to an index: value `x` belongs at index `x - 1`. The sign at that index can record whether the value has already appeared. If the target position is already negative, the current value is repeated. After the first pass, the positive position points to the missing number.

The array is changed by this method because values are made negative. That is the trade-off for using constant extra space.

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] findMissingRepeatingNumbers(int[] nums) {
        int repeating = -1;

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            int value = Math.abs(nums[i]);
            int index = value - 1;

            if (nums[index] < 0) {
                repeating = value;
            } else {
                nums[index] = -nums[index];
            }
        }

        int missing = -1;
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            if (nums[i] > 0) {
                missing = i + 1;
                break;
            }
        }

        return new int[] {repeating, missing};
    }
}
```

The `Math.abs` call matters because earlier visits may have changed some entries to negative values. The repeated number can be at either end of the range, and the final scan handles both cases.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space.

Kept this version close to the direct definition of the task. For each number from `1` to `n`, scan the whole list and count it. The count of `2` identifies the repeated value, while the count of `0` identifies the missing one.

Dry run with `nums = [2, 2, 3, 4]`: number `1` appears zero times, and number `2` appears twice, so the answer is `[2, 1]`.

```python
class Solution:
    def findMissingRepeatingNumbers(self, nums):
        n = len(nums)
        repeating = -1
        missing = -1

        for expected in range(1, n + 1):
            count = 0
            for value in nums:
                if value == expected:
                    count += 1

            if count == 2:
                repeating = expected
            elif count == 0:
                missing = expected

        return [repeating, missing]
```

This is slower than counting with a list or set, but it uses no extra data structure and leaves the input untouched. It also covers a missing value at the beginning or end of the range.

Time complexity: `O(n^2)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)` besides the returned list.

## Intuition
The array contains numbers from **1 to n**, where:
- One number appears **twice** (repeating).
- One number is **missing**.

To solve this:
1. Use a **hash map** to find the repeating number by counting the frequency of each element.
2. Use a **set** to store only unique elements, then compare the expected sum of numbers from `1` to `n` with the actual sum of the unique elements to determine the missing number.

---

## Algorithm

### Finding the Repeating Number
1. Create an unordered map to store the frequency of each element.
2. Traverse the array and increment the frequency of each number.
3. Traverse the map and return the element whose frequency is greater than `1`.

### Finding the Missing Number
1. Insert all elements into a set to remove duplicates.
2. Compute the expected sum of numbers from `1` to `n`:
   ![](https://static.takeuforward.org/content/1784267958_tWvCOFXJ.png)
3. Compute the sum of all unique elements in the set.
4. The difference between the expected sum and the actual sum gives the missing number.

---

## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution {
private:
    int repeating_number(vector<int>& nums){
        int n = nums.size();
        // hash map
        unordered_map<int,int> mpp;
        // pre-compute
        for(auto i: nums){
            mpp[i]++;
        }
        // map traversal
        for(auto i: mpp){
            if(i.second > 1){
                return i.first;
            }
        }
        return -1;
    }

    int missing_element(vector<int>& nums){
        int n = nums.size();

        //putting elements in a set 
        set<int> st;
        for(auto i: nums){
            st.insert(i);
        }

        int expected_sum = n * (n + 1) / 2;
        int actual_sum = 0;
        for(auto i: st){
            actual_sum += i;
        }
        return expected_sum - actual_sum;
    }
public:
    vector<int> findMissingRepeatingNumbers(vector<int> nums) {
        int n = nums.size();
        vector<int> temp;       // repeating, missing

        int a = repeating_number(nums);
        int b = missing_element(nums);

        temp.push_back(a);
        temp.push_back(b);

        return temp;
    }
};
```

## Complexity
Time Complexity: `O(n log n)`
Auxiliary Space Complexity: `O(n)`

